---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
const rawBase = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
const base = rawBase.endsWith('/') ? rawBase : rawBase + '/';
---
<Base
  title="The web UI — Streamsemble"
  description="One page controls the whole hub: pick speakers live, set volumes, watch the sync, and run the lights — from any browser on your network."
  current="webui"
>
  <main class="doc wrap">
    <header>
      <h1>The web UI</h1>
      <p class="doc__lede">
        Open <code>http://&lt;your hub&gt;:8088</code> from any phone or laptop
        on the network. Everything the hub does is on this one page, live.
      </p>
    </header>

    <section aria-labelledby="speakers">
      <h2 id="speakers">Speakers</h2>
      <p>
        The page lists every AirPlay speaker it can see on the network — found
        automatically, nothing to register. Tick one and it joins the music
        within a few seconds, already in time with the rest; untick it and it
        drops out. You can do this mid-song, whenever you like.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="volume">
      <h2 id="volume">Volume</h2>
      <p>
        Each speaker gets its own slider, and the reading comes from the
        speaker itself — turn a Sonos up from its own app and the slider here
        moves too. The big slider at the top is the average of them all; move
        it and every speaker follows.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="nowplaying">
      <h2 id="nowplaying">Now playing</h2>
      <p>
        The active source, track, artist, and cover art. The artwork shown is
        the same image the hub sends to the speakers' own displays — if it's
        blank here, the speakers got nothing either, which makes it a useful
        honesty check.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="sync">
      <h2 id="sync">The sync view</h2>
      <p>
        A small live graphic shows each speaker's buffer lead against its
        target, with a short history. You'll rarely need it — sync is
        automatic — but when something sounds off, it lets you see which
        speaker is struggling instead of guessing.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="lights">
      <h2 id="lights">Lights</h2>
      <p>
        If you've configured WLED strips (see
        <a href={`${base}advanced/`}>Advanced setup</a>), a Lights card
        appears: per-strip on/off, the light mode (Pulse, Vu, or Spectrum), a
        palette (Classic, Solid, or Rainbow), color, brightness, how slowly the
        lights fall after a beat, and mirror/reverse for how the show sits on
        the strip. Changes land on the strip within about a frame — you can
        tune it while the music plays.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="api">
      <h2 id="api">The API underneath</h2>
      <p>
        Every control on the page is a plain HTTP call you can use yourself:
      </p>
      <pre is:raw><code>GET  /api/state              # everything: speakers, volumes, telemetry, lights
POST /api/targets            # which speakers play
POST /api/volume             # all speakers
POST /api/speakers/volume    # one speaker
POST /api/wled/config        # light mode, palette, brightness…</code></pre>
      <p>
        If you want to script the house — a button by the bed that moves the
        music to the bedroom, say — it's all there.
      </p>
    </section>

    <p class="next">
      <a href={`${base}advanced/`}>Next: advanced setup →</a>
    </p>
  </main>
</Base>
